\n- Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 2026 to 165bp, so borrowing costs more.\n- The market is still open. Norvane Utilities priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably, and its 2027 capex is up 12%. Norvane looks financeable through debt.\n\n**Equity: mostly no.**\n- Equity markets were \"less receptive\" in Q3 2026, and two utility share offerings were cut back in size.\n- Pellham Power withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short. Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria, and its 2027 capex plan is up 18%.\n\n**Pellham is the weak point.**\n- It said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves.\n- Pellham is cutting spending instead of switching to debt. That suggests debt alone can't cover its plan, probably because of balance-sheet or rating limits. The documents don't say this directly.\n\n**Gaps.**\n- I found no data on leverage, ratings headroom, maturity walls, valuations or the total size of 2027 funding needs.\n- I can't say how much more debt Pellham could take on.\n- Equity could reopen if conditions improve, and Pellham's wording (\"until financing improves\") implies that.\n\n**Implication.** Smaller or stronger utilities like Norvane can probably fund their plans mainly with debt. Pellham probably can't, so expect its equipment orders to fall below plan. That cuts against n5's conclusion that demand for grid equipment is rising.","ttft_ms":4647,"type":"result","duration_ms":39793,"uuid":"f10a551c-179a-44b1-8707-b1bd2a5c47ca","ttft_stream_ms":3801,"time_to_request_ms":108,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":7,"system_prompt":10,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":8,"input_attachments":37,"input_hooks":7,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":5,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":4,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1